From 90eb929c6fb0bfa90cb65aa56961d6df5f97bea4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Boyd <danieljboyd@icloud.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 07:29:38 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 14/15] win32: hide, clip and destroy an OpenGL view's child
 window with the view

An NSOpenGLView's drawable is a child window over the GNUstep window, which
Windows draws whatever the views around it do.  Win32Subwindow only moved
it, so a hidden view, a view in a hidden ancestor, and the part of a view
scrolled out of a scroll view all stayed on screen.  -update now hides the
child window unless the view is visible, and clips it to the view's
visible rectangle with a window region.  The window is created hidden and
shown by -update.

Detaching a context left the child window on screen until the subwindow
object was deallocated, which an autorelease pool can put off; it is now
destroyed when the context detaches.  -setView: made the new child window
before releasing the old one, so the old window's WM_DESTROY deleted the
new window's WGL context; the old window now goes first, and WM_DESTROY
clears the handle it deletes.

The placement code for -initWithView: and -update is shared, unchanged.

